{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791126022,"nonce":"yznfykKuyuDDXgb5AASrYMcrUHw=","message":"Wired into the render path.\n\nThe epoch is bumped once per TEXT WALK, not per frame, in\ncollect_render_elements_with_state. That distinction is the safety\nproperty: a cached background batch serves frames without re-preparing\nits text, so those glyphs go untouched. Counting frames would age out\nglyphs still on screen; counting walks ages a glyph only when a walk\nthat could have drawn it did not. A static UI served from cache never\nadvances the epoch and never loses a glyph.\n\nThe GC runs at the start of a walk, before anything is touched, so the\nprevious walk's glyphs are one epoch old and safe. It runs only above\n85% shelf utilization, since below that the atlas has room and a GC\nwould only cost re-rasterisation, and only reclaims glyphs undrawn for\n120 walks.\n\nOn reclaim it calls invalidate_static_layer, because reclaimed space is\nzeroed and reused and any cached primitive holding baked PRIM_TEXT UVs\ninto it would sample whatever lands there next.\n\n\nThe equivalent invalidation for atlas GROWTH is deliberately disabled:\ncontext.rs discards take_atlas_grew()'s result. The comment there says\ninvalidating on growth stopped permanent corruption but caused per-frame\nblink during continuous zoom, because every smooth-zoom step rasterised\na new size, which grew the atlas, which invalidated, which forced a\nre-walk, which grew it again. The real fix named there is to rewrite\nPRIM_TEXT gradient_params at emit time against the final atlas\ndimensions. So cached batches can already carry stale UVs after growth.\nThe GC does not share that failure mode, because it fires rarely rather\nthan on every zoom step, but the underlying fragility is the same and\nworth fixing at the source.\n\n\nForcing the GC on every walk, with pressure 0.0 and max_age 0, over 8\nwalks: it reclaimed all 86 glyphs and 3 shelves every time, dropping\noccupancy to zero, and the rendered output stayed pixel-identical to the\nknown-good capture. A full evict-and-rebuild cycle produces correct\ntext.\n\nWith the shipped policy the atlas never reaches 85% in the sample\nscenes, and the rendered output is unchanged, as intended.\n\n\nThe offscreen capture path does not use the cached static layer, so the\ninvalidate-on-reclaim call is exercised only in the sense that it\ncompiles and is reached. The windowed cached-batch path has not been\npushed past 85% utilization to watch it reclaim.\n\ncn_demo cannot settle this: two captures from the SAME build, 14 seconds\nafter launch, differ by 81k pixels, so something there animates\ncontinuously and cross-launch pixel comparison is not a valid signal for\nit. An earlier 0-pixel comparison was luck.","files":null}]}